**Reused Evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["4B.10"]}
This is attribution and period-fit context only: Modi was in office through 2022.

**New Evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Explained: A stricter policy for accreditation of journalists, and the concerns","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-02-09","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/explained/stricter-policy-for-accreditation-of-journalists-and-the-concerns-7763387/","claim":"The 2022 accreditation rules introduced cancellation grounds tied to sovereignty, security, foreign relations, public order, decency, morality, defamation, and incitement.","locator":"lines 48-59","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous reporting on an official 2022 policy with specific quoted criteria; strong for the rule text, weaker for intent.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period policy evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government through the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting and PIB.","contrary_evidence":["The article notes the policy was presented as an accreditation framework rather than a press ban.","The policy could also be defended as a credentialing rule, not proof of bad-faith enforcement."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.5"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Those booked under Section 124A can approach courts for bail: Highlights from Supreme Court order on sedition law","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-05-11","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/supreme-court-sedition-centre-order-7911032/","claim":"The Supreme Court said it expected the Centre and states not to register new sedition FIRs, continue probes, or take coercive steps while the law was under review.","locator":"lines 46-55","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"A direct report of a contemporaneous Supreme Court order; high reliability for the order, but it is still secondary reporting.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period judicial constraint evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect attribution to the Modi government because the Centre was the party using and defending the sedition framework, but the immediate actor is the Court.","contrary_evidence":["This is a judicial restraint on the executive, not a direct act of censorship by Modi personally.","The government said it was reviewing the law, so the order also reflects institutional correction."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.9","4B.3"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No evidence, Govt didn’t cooperate: SC panel on Pegasus","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-08-25","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/supreme-court-pegasus-spyware-case-8110566/","claim":"The Supreme Court-appointed Pegasus panel found no conclusive evidence on the devices it examined, but the Chief Justice said the central government had not cooperated with the probe.","locator":"lines 45-46","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct reporting of the Supreme Court’s summary of the panel report; reliable for the non-cooperation finding, limited by the panel’s inability to reach conclusive technical proof.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period oversight and surveillance evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi-led central government as the entity that did not cooperate with the court-appointed probe.","contrary_evidence":["The panel did not find conclusive proof of Pegasus use on the devices it examined.","That makes the allegation unresolved rather than proven in this source alone."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.9","4B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India Should Stop Using Abusive Foreign Funding Law","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-01-18","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/01/18/india-should-stop-using-abusive-foreign-funding-law","claim":"HRW said the Indian government was harassing the Centre for Promotion of Social Concerns and People’s Watch, including through CBI action under the foreign-funding law, as part of a wider crackdown on civil society.","locator":"lines 598-608","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed human-rights reporting with named organizations, dated events, and explicit attribution to the Indian government.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period for early 2022 and squarely within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct central-government attribution through the CBI/FCRA apparatus under Modi’s administration.","contrary_evidence":["The government framed the case as fraud and foreign-funding compliance enforcement.","The report is advocacy-oriented, so its characterization of motive is not itself a legal finding."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India: Media freedom under threat","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022-05-03","url":"https://rsf.org/en/india-media-freedom-under-threat","claim":"RSF and partner groups said Indian authorities were targeting journalists and online critics under counterterrorism and sedition laws, and that India ranked 150th in the 2022 press index.","locator":"lines 328-335","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct rights-organization statement with a clear date, explicit ranking, and specific mechanisms of pressure on journalists.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period press-freedom evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the BJP-led central government and its enforcement choices; some harassment also came from aligned actors.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is a campaign from rights organizations, not a court finding.","The government can argue it was enforcing security and public-order laws."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/india","claim":"The 2022 country report says the Kashmir Press Club was forcibly taken over in January by people allegedly close to local administration, and that new accreditation rules could suspend journalists for work deemed harmful to sovereignty or national security.","locator":"lines 43-45","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Institutional journalism research with explicit 2022 country findings; strong for media-environment context, though the survey portion is not nationally representative.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is partly direct and partly indirect: the central ministry issued the accreditation rules, while the takeover involved local administration and armed police.","contrary_evidence":["The report is partly survey-based and limited to English-speaking online users for some metrics.","The Kashmir Press Club episode involves alleged local-administration proximity rather than a court-tested national order."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India's ruling party asks officials to exercise caution on religious issues after Islamic nations protest","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-06-07","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/indias-ruling-party-asks-officials-to-exercise-caution-on-religious-issues-after-islamic-nations-protest-2834432","claim":"Reuters reported that BJP leaders instructed officials to be extremely cautious about religious remarks after Prophet-related comments triggered protests, and said Muslims were under pressure on worship and hijab issues.","locator":"lines 419-423","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on party instructions and the surrounding backlash; strong for the reported directive, weaker for broader causal interpretation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Party-level attribution is direct to the BJP, and indirectly to Modi as the party’s national leader and government head.","contrary_evidence":["The instruction was framed as cautionary messaging rather than overt repression.","It does not by itself prove direct Modi personal authorship of the remarks."],"lenses":["4B.7","4B.5","4B.3"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India rushes to quell outrage after insulting remarks on Islam","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-06-07","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/06/07/us-india-politics-diplomacy","claim":"Reuters reported that the government sought to calm anger at home and abroad after ruling-party remarks on the Prophet, and that 38 people were arrested for rioting in one northern city.","locator":"line 637 and surrounding paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-syndicated contemporaneous reporting with a concrete arrest count and direct government response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution is direct to the Modi administration; the triggering remarks were by BJP officials rather than Modi himself.","contrary_evidence":["The government response was calm-down and damage control, not open endorsement of violence.","The arrests were local-law-enforcement actions rather than a national decree."],"lenses":["4B.7","4B.3","4B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM Modi's party set for record win in India’s Gujarat state election","publisher":"ThePrint / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://theprint.in/india/pm-modis-party-set-for-record-win-in-indias-gujarat-state-election/1255369/","claim":"Reuters reported that Modi’s BJP was headed for a record Gujarat win, with the party on course for its biggest majority in the state and a strong boost before 2024.","locator":"headline and lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct election-result reporting with clear target-year fit; useful for contestability context, but a win alone does not prove electoral fairness.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period election evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is indirect but real: this is Modi’s home-state party and his national political base, not a personal electoral decree.","contrary_evidence":["A large victory does not by itself prove clean competition.","This source is outcome context, not evidence that Modi respected or violated rules."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Himachal Pradesh results: Difference of 37,974 votes made all the difference for Congress over BJP","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-12-09","url":"https://indianexpress.com/elections/himachal-pradesh-verdict-2022-difference-of-37974-votes-made-a-lot-of-difference-for-cong-bjp-8314460/","claim":"The Congress displaced the BJP in Himachal Pradesh by a margin of 37,974 votes, with both parties contesting the state intensely and the BJP conceding defeat.","locator":"lines 47-48 and 63-65","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous results reporting with vote-share and seat detail; strong for election contestability, though state-level rather than national.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period election evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution to Modi is indirect but meaningful: his party campaigned heavily and still lost an incumbent state government.","contrary_evidence":["This is a state election, not a national transfer of power.","The result is evidence that contestability remained real, not that Modi personally strengthened institutions."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM Narendra Modi and accusations of poll code violation: From 2014 to Congress complaint today","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-12-05","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/political-pulse/pm-narendra-modi-poll-code-violation-congress-complaint-8307501/","claim":"Congress complained to the Election Commission that Modi’s walk after voting in Ahmedabad amounted to a roadshow, and the EC dismissed the complaint as not established.","locator":"lines 45-53","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct reporting on a live electoral complaint and the EC’s response; reliable for the dispute, but the alleged violation was not upheld.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period election evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Modi personally, but only as an allegation; the EC rejection means it is evidence of contestation and oversight rather than a proven violation.","contrary_evidence":["The Election Commission rejected the claim that a roadshow had been established.","This is therefore better read as a contested complaint than as confirmed manipulation."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Election Commission asks parties to explain how they plan to finance poll promises, draws Opposition fire","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-10-05","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/ec-asks-parties-to-explain-how-they-plan-to-finance-poll-promises-draws-opposition-fire-8190263/","claim":"The Election Commission asked parties to disclose how they would finance election promises and why, aiming to standardize information for voters and inform choice.","locator":"lines 46-55 and 65-69","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct reporting on an EC consultation paper and the resulting party criticism; useful institutional context, though not itself a ruler act.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period electoral-governance evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect attribution only: it reflects the electoral environment under Modi’s tenure, not a Modi personal decision.","contrary_evidence":["The move drew criticism as overreach from opposition parties.","It is an EC process, not proof that Modi himself was either restrained or manipulative."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.4","4B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ministry of I&B blocks 16 YouTube news channels for spreading disinformation related to India’s national security, foreign relations and public order","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-04-25","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1819892&reg=48&lang=2","claim":"The Ministry of Information and Broadcasting used emergency powers under the IT Rules to block 16 YouTube news channels and one Facebook account, saying they spread fake news on security, foreign relations, communal harmony, and public order.","locator":"lines 11-19","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official release with a precise date, concrete blocking action, and the government’s stated justification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Direct central-government attribution under the Modi administration through the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting.","contrary_evidence":["The government framed the action as anti-disinformation and public-order protection.","The source itself does not prove the blocked content was genuinely disinformation."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.2"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across official releases, court reporting, Reuters-syndicated coverage, rights reports, and journalism-research material. I retained 11 new source-claim units plus one reused attribution item.

Rejected or collapsed categories:
- Duplicate syndications of the same Reuters story through multiple hosts were collapsed to one unit.
- Image-only Reuters Connect items were rejected when they added no substantive claim beyond the caption.
- Out-of-period 2023-2024 retrospectives were skipped unless they directly described a 2022 action that could be dated back to the target year.
- Pure commentary, opinion, and non-specific explainers were rejected when they did not add ruler-period facts.
- I did not re-fetch the local structured datasets already supplied in the prompt.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct 2022 evidence of Modi personally ordering electoral manipulation is thin. The record is stronger on BJP-led or central-government mechanisms than on a documented personal directive.
- The strongest affirmative institutional evidence is mostly that elections and oversight still functioned, not that Modi materially strengthened those institutions in 2022.
- Several adverse items are indirect or party-level rather than an explicit Modi order, so attribution remains mixed.
- More search would help on central-media licensing, EC enforcement decisions, and any dated ministerial instructions that tie pressure more tightly to Modi personally.
